**Threads printing in order.** The two threads must take turns: odd prints 1, even prints 2, odd prints 3, and so on. A shared flag says whose turn it is, and each thread waits until it is its turn.

```java
class Printer {
    private int next = 1;
    private final int max;
    Printer(int max) { this.max = max; }

    synchronized void print(boolean odd) throws InterruptedException {
        while (next <= max) {
            while (next <= max && (next % 2 == 1) != odd) wait();
            if (next > max) break;
            System.out.println(next++);
            notifyAll();
        }
        notifyAll();
    }
}
// Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> p.print(true));  Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> p.print(false));
```

How it works: both threads share one `Printer` object. A thread holds the monitor, checks the turn in a **`while` loop** (not `if`, to guard against spurious wakeups), prints, increments, and calls `notifyAll()` to wake the other one. The `max` check prevents a thread from waiting forever after the last number. Alternatives: `ReentrantLock` with two `Condition`s, or two `Semaphore`s where each thread releases the other's permit after printing. The idea extends to N threads by checking `next % N`.

**If the two arrays are separate** (an odd array and an even array), each thread keeps its own index and prints the next element from its array only on its turn, using the same alternating handoff.

**Kth node from the end.** Move a `fast` pointer K steps ahead, then move `fast` and `slow` together until `fast` reaches the end. `slow` is the answer. This is O(n) time, O(1) space, one pass. Check K greater than the list length.

